{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>And they shall not profane My holy name, which they sanctify to Me</b> — The verse is interrupted (סָרֵס הַמִּקְרָא) and expounded thus: \"And they shall separate themselves from the holy things of the children of Israel, which they sanctify to Me, and they shall not profane My holy name.\" <b>\"Which they sanctify to Me\"</b> — to include the holy offerings of the priests themselves; this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Leviticus 22:2). But if we interpret \"which they sanctify to Me\" as referring to the priests themselves, there is no need for this interruption of the verse; rather, Scripture would say: \"And they shall separate themselves from the holy things of the children of Israel, and they shall not profane My holy name through them,\" and \"which they sanctify to Me\" — meaning they shall not profane that which Aaron and his sons themselves sanctify to Me. And in Torat Kohanim (parashah 4, mishnah 1) it is expounded thus: \"And they shall separate themselves from the holy things of the children of Israel\" — for the holy things of Israel they are liable on account of piggul, notar, and tumah, but they are not liable for the holy things of Gentiles on account of piggul, notar, and tumah. \"The holy things of Israel\" — I have only the holy things of Israel; how do I know the holy things of themselves? Scripture teaches: \"which they sanctify to Me, I am YHWH\" — to include them all. And no interruption of the verse is mentioned there at all. And by way of truth, \"which they sanctify to Me, I am YHWH\" — that they shall not profane the Name sanctified to Him, for His name is in the sanctuary of YHWH. And I have already hinted at this in the pericasha of \"And they shall take for Me a contribution\" (Exodus 25:2)."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:57:36.955565676Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}